\title{A Constructive, Non-Circular Approximate Intermediate Value Theorem with Explicit Modulus}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-21}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The classical Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) asserts that a continuous function changing sign on an interval has a root, but its standard proofs are non-constructive: they rely on the completeness of the reals (via a least-upper-bound) or on the topological connectedness of intervals, and they yield a root that may be uncomputable. Following the constructive program of Errett Bishop, we develop an \emph{approximate} IVT that, given a tolerance $\varepsilon > 0$ and an explicit modulus of continuity for the function, locates a point $x$ in the interval with $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$. The development is organized strictly bottom-up and is deliberately \emph{non-circular}: it does not invoke the classical IVT, connectedness, preconnectedness, or any root-existence theorem. The analytic content is reduced to a single finite, order-theoretic lemma --- \texttt{finite\_sign\_change} --- which states that a finite list of reals running from non-positive to non-negative either hits zero or exhibits an adjacent sign change. From this we derive a discrete approximate IVT (\texttt{discrete\_approx\_ivt}), then an oriented modulus-based theorem on a uniform grid (\texttt{approx\_ivt\_of\_modulus\_nonpos\_nonneg}), and finally a symmetric version handling either sign orientation (\texttt{approx\_ivt\_of\_modulus}). We describe the grid construction, prove the supporting mesh estimates, present the algorithm and its complexity, discuss applications to certified numerical root-finding, and outline generalizations to bisection refinement and the multidimensional Poincar\'e--Miranda setting. All results have been formalized and machine-checked.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

The Intermediate Value Theorem is among the first deep results a student of analysis meets:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{(Classical IVT.)} If $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous and $f(a) \le 0 \le f(b)$, then there exists $c \in [a,b]$ with $f(c) = 0$.
\end{quote}

Despite its intuitive obviousness, the theorem is not constructively valid in its classical form. The obstruction is fundamental: equality and order on the real numbers are \emph{not decidable}. Determining whether a real number is exactly zero, as opposed to extremely small but nonzero, would require examining infinitely much information. As a consequence, one can construct continuous functions whose unique sign-change point is not computable by any algorithm; the classical existence claim then has no algorithmic content.

Bishop's constructive analysis resolves this not by abandoning the theorem but by \emph{reformulating the conclusion} into something an algorithm can deliver. Rather than demanding an exact root, we demand an \emph{approximate} one:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{(Approximate IVT, informal.)} For every tolerance $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $x \in [a,b]$ with $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{quote}

This conclusion is constructively provable, and its proof is an explicit terminating search, \emph{provided} continuity is supplied in a quantitative form --- a \emph{modulus of continuity} --- rather than the classical $\varepsilon$--$\delta$ form with a non-computable $\delta$.

\subsection{Contribution}

We present a fully formalized, bottom-up development of the approximate IVT with the following distinguishing features:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Explicit modulus.} Continuity enters only through a pointwise uniform modulus hypothesis: there is a step size $\delta$ such that inputs within $\delta$ have outputs within $\varepsilon$.
\item \textbf{Non-circularity.} The proof uses neither the classical IVT, nor \texttt{IsPreconnected}, nor interval connectedness, nor any least-upper-bound or supremum argument. This is a design constraint, not an accident: it guarantees the result is genuinely a \emph{from-scratch} constructive theorem rather than a repackaging of a classical one.
\item \textbf{Strict separation of concerns.} The combinatorial skeleton (a finite sign change) is isolated in a lemma about sequences $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ that mentions no continuity whatsoever. The analytic content (the modulus, the grid, the metric estimates) is layered on top.
\item \textbf{An explicit, finite, terminating algorithm} with linear step count in the number of subdivisions $N$, where $N$ is determined directly from the modulus and the requested tolerance.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries and the Grid Construction}

Throughout, $a, b, \delta, \varepsilon \in \mathbb{R}$ and $N, i \in \mathbb{N}$. We work on a closed interval $[a,b]$ with $a \le b$.

\subsection{The uniform grid}

\begin{definition}[Grid]
The \emph{uniform grid} on $[a,b]$ with $N$ subdivisions assigns to index $i$ the node
\[
\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i) \;=\; a + \frac{i\,(b - a)}{N}.
\]
These are the $N+1$ equally spaced points $x_0, \dots, x_N$ partitioning $[a,b]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Endpoints]
$\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,0) = a$, and $\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,N) = b$ for $N > 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $i = 0$ the added term vanishes. For $i = N$, $\tfrac{N(b-a)}{N} = b - a$ after cancelling $N \ne 0$, and $a + (b-a) = b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Membership]
If $a \le b$, $0 < N$, and $i \le N$, then $\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i) \in [a,b]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $0 \le i \le N$ we have $0 \le \tfrac{i(b-a)}{N} \le b - a$ (using $b - a \ge 0$ and $i/N \le 1$). Adding $a$ places the node in $[a,b]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Mesh / consecutive spacing]
If $a \le b$, $0 < N$, and $\tfrac{b-a}{N} \le \delta$, then for every $i$,
\[
\bigl|\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i+1) - \operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i)\bigr| \;\le\; \delta.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The difference of consecutive nodes is exactly $\tfrac{b-a}{N} \ge 0$, so its absolute value equals $\tfrac{b-a}{N}$, which is bounded by $\delta$ by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\section{The Combinatorial Core}

The genuinely indispensable idea is a purely finite statement about sequences of reals --- no continuity, no metric, no limits.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{finite\_sign\_change}]
Let $u : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ and $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $u_0 \le 0$ and $u_N \ge 0$. Then
\[
\bigl(\exists\, i \le N,\; u_i = 0\bigr) \quad\lor\quad \bigl(\exists\, i < N,\; u_i \le 0 \;\land\; 0 \le u_{i+1}\bigr).
\]
That is, either some node is exactly zero, or there is an adjacent pair across which the sign changes from non-positive to non-negative.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Argue by contradiction: assume neither disjunct holds. Then (a) no node equals zero, and (b) no adjacent pair $(u_i, u_{i+1})$ has $u_i \le 0 \le u_{i+1}$. We prove by induction on $i$ that $u_i < 0$ for all $i \le N$. Base case: $u_0 \le 0$ and $u_0 \ne 0$ (by (a)), hence $u_0 < 0$. Inductive step: if $u_i < 0$ for $i < N$, then $u_{i+1} \ge 0$ would yield $u_i \le 0 \le u_{i+1}$, contradicting (b); so $u_{i+1} < 0$. Taking $i = N$ gives $u_N < 0$, contradicting $u_N \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{discrete\_approx\_ivt}]
Let $u : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, $N \in \mathbb{N}$, and $\varepsilon \ge 0$. Suppose $u_0 \le 0$, $u_N \ge 0$, and consecutive steps are controlled,
\[\forall\, i < N,\quad |u_{i+1} - u_i| \le \varepsilon.\]
Then there exists $i \le N$ with $|u_i| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the previous theorem. In the first case, some $u_i = 0$, so $|u_i| = 0 \le \varepsilon$. In the second case, there is $i < N$ with $u_i \le 0 \le u_{i+1}$. From $u_{i+1} \ge 0$, $|u_{i+1} - u_i| \le \varepsilon$, and $u_i \le 0$, we get $0 \le u_{i+1} = (u_{i+1} - u_i) + u_i \le u_{i+1} - u_i \le \varepsilon$. Hence $|u_{i+1}| \le \varepsilon$, with $i + 1 \le N$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Modulus-Based Approximate IVT}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{approx\_ivt\_of\_modulus\_nonpos\_nonneg}]
Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$, $a \le b$, $\varepsilon \ge 0$, $\delta > 0$, and $N > 0$, with mesh bound $\tfrac{b - a}{N} \le \delta$, endpoint signs $f(a) \le 0 \le f(b)$, and an explicit modulus of continuity on $[a,b]$:
\[
\forall\, x, y \in [a,b],\quad |y - x| \le \delta \;\Longrightarrow\; |f(y) - f(x)| \le \varepsilon.
\]
Then there exists $x \in [a,b]$ with $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Define $u_i := f(\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i))$. Then $u_0 = f(a) \le 0$ and $u_N = f(b) \ge 0$ by the endpoint lemma. For $i < N$, the nodes $\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i)$ and $\operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i+1)$ lie in $[a,b]$ and are within $\delta$, so the modulus gives $|u_{i+1} - u_i| \le \varepsilon$. By the discrete theorem there is $i \le N$ with $|u_i| \le \varepsilon$; set $x := \operatorname{grid}(a,b,N,i) \in [a,b]$, so $|f(x)| = |u_i| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[On the hypothesis $\delta > 0$]
The strict positivity $\delta > 0$ is part of the requested interface but is not actually used by the oriented proof; the mesh bound $\tfrac{b-a}{N} \le \delta$ together with the modulus hypothesis suffices. It is retained only to match the conventional statement of a modulus of continuity.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{approx\_ivt\_of\_modulus}]
Under the same hypotheses except that the sign condition is replaced by either orientation,
\[
\bigl(f(a) \le 0 \,\land\, 0 \le f(b)\bigr) \;\lor\; \bigl(0 \le f(a) \,\land\, f(b) \le 0\bigr),
\]
there exists $x \in [a,b]$ with $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If the first orientation holds, apply the oriented theorem directly. If the second holds, apply it to $g := -f$, whose endpoint signs become $g(a) \le 0 \le g(b)$ and whose modulus is identical since $|g(y) - g(x)| = |f(y) - f(x)|$. The conclusion $|g(x)| \le \varepsilon$ is exactly $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Algorithm}

\subsection{Grid search for an approximate crossing}

\textbf{Input:} interval $[a,b]$ with $a \le b$; tolerance $\varepsilon > 0$; modulus step $\delta > 0$ valid for $f$ on $[a,b]$ at tolerance $\varepsilon$; endpoint values with $f(a) \le 0 \le f(b)$ (or the reversed orientation).

\begin{enumerate}
\item Choose $N := \lceil (b-a)/\delta \rceil$ (any $N$ with $(b-a)/N \le \delta$ works), and ensure $N \ge 1$.
\item Form the $N+1$ grid nodes $x_i = a + \tfrac{i(b-a)}{N}$.
\item Evaluate (or comparably approximate) $f$ at the nodes. Find the first index $i$ with $u_i \le 0 \le u_{i+1}$ (or an exact zero $u_i = 0$).
\item Return that boundary node (the non-negative member of the straddling pair, or the exact zero).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Output:} a node $x \in [a,b]$ with the certified guarantee $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$.

\subsection{Complexity}

The search inspects at most $N + 1$ grid points and performs $O(N)$ comparisons, with $N = \lceil (b-a)/\delta \rceil$. The cost is linear in the ratio of the interval length to the modulus step. Because $\delta$ is determined by the function's steadiness at the requested tolerance $\varepsilon$, the entire cost is an explicit, finite function of $(a, b, \varepsilon, \delta)$.

\subsection{On decidability of the comparisons}

In exact real arithmetic, the comparison $u_i \le 0 \le u_{i+1}$ is not decidable. However, the algorithm never needs the \emph{exact} sign of $f$ at a node --- only a comparison accurate to within $\varepsilon$. A certified inexact comparator (e.g.\ a rational/dyadic approximation of $f$ with a known error bound below $\varepsilon$) is sufficient to drive the finite search.

\section{Discussion: Why Non-Circularity Matters}

The classical IVT is typically proved via the least-upper-bound principle, taking $c = \sup\{x : f(x) \le 0\}$, or via connectedness. Both proofs are non-constructive: the supremum need not be computable, and connectedness exhibits no point. The development here deliberately avoids both routes, using no classical IVT, no \texttt{IsPreconnected}, no completeness, and no root-existence theorem. The only nontrivial existence step is the finite sign-change lemma, whose proof is a finite induction over tested order relations. This makes the construction genuinely foundational and conceptually transparent: classical proofs hide \emph{where} the crossing comes from inside an abstract supremum, whereas the grid proof shows exactly where --- at the unique adjacent pair where the sampled sequence turns over.

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Certified numerical root-finding.} Every practical root-finder returns a point where $|f(x)|$ is small, not a provably exact root. The symmetric theorem is the rigorous specification such routines meet.

\textbf{Approximate equation solving.} To solve $f(x) = t$ approximately, apply the theorem to $g(x) = f(x) - t$; the modulus of $g$ equals that of $f$.

\textbf{Teaching constructive analysis.} The proof separates a transparent finite lemma from analytic packaging, illustrating how Bishop-style mathematics replaces non-constructive existence with explicit search.

\section{Future Directions}

\textbf{A fully computable approximate-root finder with verified complexity.} Parametrizing the search by a decidable sign oracle (a rational/dyadic approximation of $f$ with a known error bound) would yield an executable \texttt{findApproxRoot} together with a proof that its output satisfies $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$ and an $O(N)$ step count. The key insight is that the approximate IVT never needs the exact sign of $f$ at a grid point, only a comparison accurate to within $\varepsilon$.

\textbf{Logarithmic-depth bisection refinement.} A bisection variant that repeatedly halves the bracketing interval would give an $O(\log N)$ approximate root at the same accuracy, while still avoiding any exact-zero claim. The finite sign-change lemma already isolates a single straddling adjacent pair; applying the same minimization recursively on halves turns the one-shot grid into a constructive bisection with an explicit contraction rate.

\textbf{Multidimensional generalization (Poincar\'e--Miranda).} An approximate, constructive version on a product grid would generalize this work. The higher-dimensional case reduces to a finite combinatorial lemma on a triangulated grid (a Sperner-style labeling) that, like \texttt{finite\_sign\_change}, carries no analytic content.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a constructive, non-circular, machine-checked approximate intermediate value theorem with an explicit modulus of continuity. The argument reduces all analytic existence to a single finite order lemma (\texttt{finite\_sign\_change}), lifts it to sequences (\texttt{discrete\_approx\_ivt}), and transfers it to continuous functions via a uniform grid with elementary mesh estimates, yielding both an oriented theorem (\texttt{approx\_ivt\_of\_modulus\_nonpos\_nonneg}) and a symmetric one (\texttt{approx\_ivt\_of\_modulus}). The result is an explicit terminating algorithm of linear cost whose accuracy is contracted in advance by the requested tolerance.

